    22	4. **Check behavior and adaptation.** Test relevant clicks, hover, scroll, navigation, forms, menus, animation states, keyboard access, Arabic RTL, mobile safe areas, and representative tablet/desktop widths. Check overflow, clipping, crops, wraps, spacing, and tile seams. Use [docs/mobile.md](docs/mobile.md). **Exit:** required states work at the sizes they support.
    23	5. **Review and optimize.** For code changes, run Codex's native `/review` on the actual diff before delivery (CLI equivalent when needed), examine every actionable finding, fix it, and rerun affected checks. Run available type, lint, test, and production-build checks. Review component boundaries, duplication, accessibility, and unused code. Measure first render, LCP/INP/CLS where measurable, network bytes, JavaScript, layout shifts, and scroll smoothness in a production build. Reassess static, request-time server, and client rendering against measured latency, freshness, and client cost; change strategy where it improves the result. Fix meaningful problems, then repeat affected visual/browser checks. Use [QUALITY.md](QUALITY.md) and [PERFORMANCE.md](PERFORMANCE.md). **Exit:** no known material code, visual, accessibility, or speed regression remains; report if `/review` was unavailable rather than counting a manual pass as its result.
    24	6. **Learn and report evidence.** Complete the affected [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) entries with actual checks, technical approval and remaining open IDs. Refresh [MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md) when verified state, decisions, or blockers changed; record repeatable errors in [LESSONS.md](LESSONS.md). Use [docs/definition-of-done.md](docs/definition-of-done.md). State tested viewports, screenshot comparisons, interactions, build checks, performance measurements, remaining differences, and checks that could not run. Never claim pixel fidelity, WCAG compliance, a 95+ Lighthouse score, or completion without the corresponding evidence.
    25
    26	**Do not mark a failed gate passed or delegate ordinary QA to the user.** Route the failure back to the stage that can fix it, repeat that stage, and rerun downstream checks the fix could affect. A blocked external dependency must not stop independent work. For UI work, the implement → browser screenshot → compare → fix → recheck loop is mandatory after each completed visual slice and after later appearance changes; repeat it on the assembled page. Work autonomously through all feasible checks; resolve routine choices from the repository and references, and seek only irreducible external facts or access while continuing independent work. For a small non-UI task, apply only relevant stages.
    27
    28	For substantive project work, start one independent, non-editing **discipline auditor** alongside scope inspection. Reuse it at each applicable stage gate: scope before implementation; each completed UI slice's rendering and visual evidence; behavior/adaptation; code review and performance; and final integration, memory, and delivery evidence. Give it acceptance checks, changed files, browser images, command results, and any other agents' findings as relevant. Wait for its evidence-based pass/fail/blocked finding before closing a gate. Fix failures and re-audit affected gates; the main agent owns implementation and required checks. Use the project [auditor profile](.codex/agents/discipline_auditor.toml) when available and [supervision protocol](docs/supervision.md); if the profile cannot be selected, give a subagent the same non-editing assignment. Do not spawn it for a trivial edit. The auditor supplements real QA and native `/review`; it cannot certify missing evidence.
    29
    30	## Standing constraints
    31
    32	- Git: work on `main`. Create or switch to another working branch only on the user’s explicit command; do not infer authorization from the configured upstream, skill defaults or an old checkout. Check the current branch and exact destination before commit/push; push to `origin/main` for this project.
    33
    34	- Working-context efficiency: throughout coding and QA, read only affected code and stage docs; inspect the long reference and screenshots through relevant crops at useful resolution. Keep full-size images, raw image data, long logs, and unrelated file dumps out of model context. Reuse recorded findings, bound tool output, and rerun only checks affected by a change. Never skip required QA to save tokens; stop once all applicable gates pass.
    35	- Primary devices: **iPhone 17 Pro (402 × 874 CSS px, DPR 3)** is the baseline; **iPhone 17 Pro Max (440 × 956 CSS px, DPR 3)** is the second required mobile target. These are emulation screen sizes, not fixed-height CSS requirements; Safari's visible area changes with browser chrome. Build mobile-first for these devices. All other phones, tablets, and desktops receive responsive adaptation, with no horizontal overflow or broken content. Preserve the supplied composition rather than substituting a generic design. See [docs/mobile.md](docs/mobile.md).
    36	- Locales: English, Russian, German, French, Spanish, Italian, Turkish, Arabic, Chinese, Japanese, and Korean. Use translation keys. Arabic needs correct RTL layout and behavior.
    37	- Accessibility: semantic content, keyboard and screen reader support, labels, visible focus, usable touch targets, WCAG AA contrast, and reduced-motion support. Verify affected behavior.
    38	- Product truth: the render is the visual/content target, not proof of prices, certifications, health claims, stock, or backend behavior. Preserve its visible narrative in the local rebuild; track unverified facts and confirm them before publication or functioning commerce. See [PRODUCT.md](PRODUCT.md).
    39	- Performance: the first viewport must become useful quickly. The two reference PNGs are each about 44 MB; never serve either as one production background. Derive responsive, compressed sections/tiles from the clean artwork, load the visible segment eagerly and later segments near the viewport, and avoid seams or blank gaps. Never lazy-load the LCP asset. Aim for 95+ mobile Lighthouse only with measured evidence.
    40	- Artwork color: **always load [artwork-color-pipeline](.agents/skills/artwork-color-pipeline/SKILL.md)** before adding or replacing Photoshop/raster references, cropping or resizing them for the site, converting PNG/PSD exports to WebP/AVIF, or changing how those raster assets are delivered. Apply its source-profile, decoded-color, alpha, browser and speed checks before delivery. CSS-only photo sizing/object-fit/fullscreen adaptation also loads its geometry contract; it does not require raster conversion. Ordinary text and vector-only edits do not trigger it.
    41	- Tooling: prefer native Codex capabilities when they provide the required evidence; use additional skills/tools for project-specific workflow or capabilities the native route lacks. Read the project [implementer skill](.agents/skills/pixel-perfect-ui-implementer/SKILL.md) when building UI and the [testing skill](.agents/skills/pixel-perfect-ui-testing/SKILL.md) when verifying it. Use the Codex browser for quick exploratory inspection when available; use the configured Playwright WebKit/DPR 3 profiles for required mobile screenshots and repeatable checks. `/review` is a native Codex command, not an installable skill. Load each skill when its stage needs it, not both up front. Broad design-generation skills must not replace the approved render. The requested Codex model and modes are session settings: check availability before claiming they are active. See [TOOLS.md](TOOLS.md) for other skills and limitations.
    42	- Self-improvement: after **every user correction and meaningful self-detected failure**, find the root cause, fix and recheck it, then decide whether the prevention generalizes. If repeatable, update [LESSONS.md](LESSONS.md) with **problem → cause → prevention** and consult the relevant lesson before similar work; do not promote one-off feedback into a global rule. Keep [MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md) current with verified project state and decisions, without duplicating the task transcript. Preserve user edits and design sources.
    43
    44	If a required check is blocked, explain the concrete reason and do not present it as passed. Do not stop at the first implementation when the remaining stages can be completed.

